WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.126 --> 00:00:03.043
(electronic music)

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.080
- What's up guys, Michael
here to talk about people,

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.070
but make them small and bald.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:10.470
That's right, babies.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.810
And specifically, is it good to have them?

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.057
Because increasingly, people
are starting to think,

00:00:15.057 --> 00:00:16.280
"Not so much."

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:17.470
Recently, public figures

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.240
like Prince Harry and Meghan Markle

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.060
or Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez
are starting to argue

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.810
that having fewer or even zero children

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.120
might be the most moral

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.510
and/or most responsible thing to do.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:30.500
Of course, for folks who've always dreamed

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.180
of birthing a little tyke
or two or a baker's dozen,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.570
contemplating the idea
of not bringing life

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:37.400
to an adorable monster like this

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.200
is understandably upsetting.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.630
So, it's worth asking:

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:42.910
is having a kid, or even a lot of them,

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.020
a positive way to pass down
your superb head of hair

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:46.853
and green thumb?

00:00:46.853 --> 00:00:49.330
Or is there something
irresponsible about bringing life

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:52.150
into this slowly sinking
Titanic of a planet?

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.410
Let's find out in this Wisecrack Edition:

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:56.430
Is Having Babies Ethical?

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:59.360
So, how has procreation
historically been regarded?

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.190
Well, Christian morality has long held

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.220
that having children, if
you can, is a good thing.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.700
In the Bible, God tells a bunch of people,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.930
like Adam and Eve, and also Noah

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.080
to be fruitful and multiply.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.620
And obviously, some people
do still take this imperative

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:14.860
very seriously.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:15.730
If you don't believe us,

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:17.240
go meet some Midwestern Catholics

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.490
or just rent "Cheaper
by the Dozen 1 and 2."

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:20.870
But it's also an imperative

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:22.690
that's been challenged by philosophers.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.000
For instance, take Schopenhauer,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.720
a crotchety pessimist who thought

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.120
that we may as well be living

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.870
in the worst of all possible worlds.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:31.980
And he didn't even live

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.940
to see the season finale of "Hawkeye."

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.210
He believed reproduction

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.380
was little more than an
unwelcome side effect

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.670
of humankind's endlessly failed attempts

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.350
to satisfy our desires.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.330
A moment of physical pleasure, then,

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:46.500
i.e. doing sex could result in offspring,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.380
thus continuing the human race

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.090
and the misery of its existence

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.090
via kids that will grow up and
be just as miserable as you.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.027
It's like your middle school
health teacher told you,

00:01:56.027 --> 00:01:58.480
"Sex has consequences."

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.890
Meanwhile, in his book,
"The Birth of Tragedy,"

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:04.060
Nietzsche paraphrases the
Ancient Greek wisdom of Silenus,

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.740
which was given to a tragic king

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.787
by a man with the ears of a horse.

00:02:07.787 --> 00:02:10.490
"It is best not to be born at all;

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:13.710
and next to that, it is
better to die than to live."

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.130
To be clear, we do not
endorse this message

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.570
and we hope that Silenus' friends

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.760
gave him a big hug after he wrote that.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.670
Philosophers call this view,
that it is bad to be born

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:25.550
or to have children, antinatalism.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.660
The most prominent contemporary
proponent of antinatalism

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.510
is the South African
philosopher David Benatar.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.860
We talked about him in our
video on "Attack on Titan,"

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.420
if you wanna check that out too.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.080
Benatar, like Schopenhauer,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.000
thinks the world is really, really bad.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.100
But interestingly,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.890
he argues that it would
still be morally wrong

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.040
to bring new human life into existence

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.820
even if the world wasn't a
greenhouse filled with farts.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.340
See, Benatar thinks that
there is an asymmetry

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.240
between pleasure and pain.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.900
We all know it feels good
to experience pleasure

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.610
and bad to experience
pain, but Benatar argues,

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.250
it's only bad not to experience pleasure

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.630
if you are conscious that
you are being deprived

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:05.640
of said pleasure.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.230
Like, if you didn't know

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.620
that this 24-karat gold ice cream existed,

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:10.880
you couldn't be sad

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.930
that you will likely
never have the pleasure

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:13.910
of indulging in it.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.330
But now you know about it

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.320
and we can all be depressed together.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:20.700
Conversely, it's good not to be in pain

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.000
even if we're not conscious
of that lack of pain at all.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.330
We don't have to be thinking
about how (beep) it might feel

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:27.780
to sit on a cactus

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.800
to be enjoying the state
of non-cactus sitting.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.060
But now you are, so you're welcome.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.720
Thus, the asymmetry
between pain and pleasure.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.570
bringing people into existence, then,

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:39.910
gives them the ability to
feel pleasure and pain.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.730
And this, for Benatar, is morally bad

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.270
because no amounts of pleasure

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.760
could ever outweigh the
wrong we've done to someone

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.110
by giving them the ability to feel pain.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.650
So, thanks mom.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:53.490
As it happens, Benatar
argues our own world

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:56.597
is characterized more by
pain than it is by pleasure.

00:03:56.597 --> 00:03:57.970
And if you don't believe us,

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.070
just ask Ben Affleck's face.

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:02.390
But even in a hypothetical world

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:04.300
where everyone only experienced pleasure,

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.610
it still wouldn't be right

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.240
to bring someone into
existence, says Benatar.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.260
That's because he thinks such a life

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.300
would be about as good as a
state of total non-existence.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:16.850
For this reason, Benatar
argues having kids

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:19.550
can never be anything
other than a selfish act.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:22.290
At one point in his book,
"Better to Have Never Been:

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.040
The Harm of Coming into Existence,"

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:28.160
Benatar even compares having
children to taking hostages

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.020
as people can use their children

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.110
to leverage better treatment in society.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:35.150
One of his examples is if
two women need a kidney,

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:36.590
but there is only one available

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:38.990
and one of those women is a
mother and the other is not,

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.360
who does society thinks
should get the kidney?

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.130
But people aren't all questioning

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:44.480
the morality of baby-making

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.470
because they've read David Benatar.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:47.460
For the most part,

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.570
it's because they've
been watching the news.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:52.110
With this crappery
greeting us every night,

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:55.410
it's no wonder one in four
child-free American adults

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.040
say they plan not to have children

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.610
because of climate change.

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:00.520
As we all hopefully know,

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.070
the climate disaster is being
caused by the pollution,

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:06.130
in particular, the carbon
emissions of human beings.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.250
It therefore makes sense

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:10.340
that reducing the number of
humans would help the crisis,

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.860
especially the number of
humans in developed countries

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:15.070
who are responsible for
the bulk of emissions.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:17.270
For more on how to fix the climate crisis,

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:20.520
check out our Wisecrack Labs
video on carbon capture.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.910
Over the past 200 years,

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:24.630
the Earth's population has
gone from 1 billion people

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.580
to 8 billion, and it's only going up.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.640
Perhaps it would be right
to see this sort of growth

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.180
as unsustainable.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:34.380
Now, Exxon is obviously
way more responsible

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:35.860
for this large-scale pollution

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:37.650
than you and your Honda Civic.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:40.000
Still, individual solutions for combating

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.290
or at least mitigating
climate change to some extent

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:44.900
have become more well-known.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.810
We're all aware we could be
reducing our carbon emissions

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.100
by eating less meat, flying less often,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.110
or investing in an electric car.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:53.780
Though, not a Tesla.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.630
Please, never a Tesla.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:58.440
We know that we should be
buying zero-waste products

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.840
or conscientiously sorting our recycling,

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.600
but empirical studies have found that

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:04.700
of all of the climate-friendly changes,

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.800
by far, the most effective would be

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.640
don't make any new little
polluting machines.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.830
In other words, have fewer kids.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.690
If you get rid of your car, for instance,

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:16.490
you might save 2.4 tons of carbon a year,

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:19.410
but if you have one fewer
child, studies have argued,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.910
you save on average 58.6 tons a year.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:25.640
This is what Harry and
Meghan are supposedly doing

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:28.280
by limiting their family
to only two children,

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:29.770
which we have to applaud.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:32.970
If the average child emits
58.6 tons of carbon a year,

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:34.170
just think of how much pollution

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.990
the average Prince emits.

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:37.500
Who knew that ending the monarchy

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.270
was such a climate-friendly move?

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.260
But having children might not be bad

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:43.570
just because they're bad for the planet.

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:45.180
Benatar goes so far as to argue

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:46.440
that having children is bad

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:49.590
for the sake of the children themselves.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:50.540
This makes sense.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:52.770
I mean, climate change?

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:55.120
Now, maybe you don't think
that existence right now

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:56.740
is necessarily a bad thing.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:59.850
I mean, you can order sushi
while you go to the bathroom

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:03.440
and we're getting to J.Lo romcoms
in the first half of 2022.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.110
But you might be able to agree

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:07.220
that existence in the
age of climate change

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:10.650
already is or might
eventually become not great.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:12.290
Even in the best-case scenarios,

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.150
life over the course
of the next few decades

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:17.060
is likely to get really
difficult for much of the planet.

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:19.160
Who's to say that a child born today

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:21.900
wouldn't eventually really
vibe with George Bailey?

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.680
- I said, I wish I'd never been born.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:24.900
- It's generally understood

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.410
that parents feel it's their duty

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:29.330
to ensure their children
have the best lives possible.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:32.100
But who can honestly make
that guarantee today?

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.820
So, this is getting pretty depressing,

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:38.420
especially if you like babies
or your name is Anne Geddes.

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:40.950
- [Anne] This is what it's all about,

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:44.250
the sense of joy that comes with babies.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:46.490
- So what hope is there?

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:49.890
Well, some philosophers
would argue, plenty.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.070
For many, arguments like Benatar's

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:54.480
don't really hold up because human beings

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.000
just don't work like that.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.340
Consider, for instance,
the views of Hannah Arendt.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.600
For Arendt, every human action
always meant a new beginning.

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:04.030
People, for the most part,

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:05.810
don't just do the same stupid things

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:08.050
over and over again for no reason,

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:09.550
unless their name is Sterling Archer.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.780
- Lana, introspection is
the enemy of happiness.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:15.970
So, my advice is don't always work for me.

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:16.883
- Has it, though?

00:08:16.883 --> 00:08:17.990
(Sterling laughs)

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:19.310
- I don't know, that's the beauty.

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:21.710
- Rather, they are constantly striving,

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:23.280
if often imperfectly,

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.640
to do something new and hopefully better.

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:28.570
And this theory of Arendt's
doesn't just apply to adults.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:30.190
It's also about children.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:31.160
Children, after all,

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.040
are never simply carbon
copies of their parents.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:35.910
They might resemble them in certain ways,

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:39.360
sharing similar experiences,
backgrounds, and proclivities,

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:40.980
but they are not identical,

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:43.679
and therefore, they are able
to do things differently.

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:47.047
Arendt used the word natality
to signify this fact:

00:08:47.047 --> 00:08:50.940
"The new beginning," as she
called it, "inherent in birth."

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:54.070
For Arendt, natality
was incredibly important

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.850
because it's what helps
save us from disaster.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:59.060
And she was making this
optimistic argument

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:01.410
after the horrors of World War II.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.440
After all, if everyone
really was identical

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:04.273
to their parents,

00:09:04.273 --> 00:09:07.240
if there was no new
beginning inherent in birth,

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:10.410
then we would always be
doomed to do the same things:

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:13.130
too set in our ways to
ever stop doing the things

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:15.880
that we know are causing
everything to go wrong.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.200
Luckily, that's not the case.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.790
I mean, just look at the
suburban teens on TikTok

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.590
posting videos about
why Frantz Fanon rips.

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:25.960
Their lame-ass parents would never.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:28.730
Because children have the
capacity to do things differently

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:30.830
and better than their
parents, Arendt argued,

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:34.270
the birth of new children is
not only a morally good thing,

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:36.640
it's our only coherent source of hope.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:39.160
Sure, things are bad now,

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:41.020
but our children are not doomed

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:42.630
to emit as much carbon as we do.

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:44.310
They can choose to be different.

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.640
Life in the age of
climate change might suck,

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.590
but that doesn't mean our children

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:50.940
will be incapable of
rising to the challenge.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:53.270
Nothing, of course, is inevitable.

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:54.870
Natality does not mean

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.830
that our children are
necessarily destined to succeed.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:00.350
They might grow up to
host a YouTube channel.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:01.550
Who put that there?

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:02.460
It's not funny.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:05.230
It's what I do and I'm proud of my job.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:06.300
I'm proud of this.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.830
But from the perspective of
the human species, at least,

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:11.400
continuing to have children,
despite everything,

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:14.490
is the only course of action
that really makes sense.

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:16.830
In this view, reproduction isn't selfish,

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:17.700
it's hopeful.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:19.410
So, what do you guys think?

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:20.840
Are children a source of hope

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:22.930
or is this just wishful thinking?

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:25.840
Is it okay to bring new
human life into existence

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.890
or would we be doing our children a favor

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:29.580
by never having them?

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.610
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:33.680
And if you're interested in
learning more about this topic,

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:36.610
check out "Infinitely Full
of Hope" by Tom Whyman,

